INSERT INTO cases (title, specialty, patient_age, patient_gender, chief_complaint, excerpt, history, examination, investigations, diagnosis, management, discussion, image_url, is_published, is_free) VALUES
('Acute Appendicitis', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Acute abdomen, Emergency, RIF pain', 'The classic ''migrating pain'' surgical emergency — don''t send it home.', 'Pain starting periumbilical, migrating to right iliac fossa (RIF) over 12–24h
Anorexia, nausea, vomiting (typically after pain onset, not before)
Low-grade fever
Ask about last menstrual period in women (to exclude ectopic/ovarian pathology)
Bowel habit change, urinary symptoms (to rule out differentials)', 'Tenderness and guarding at McBurney''s point
Rebound tenderness, Rovsing''s sign, psoas sign, obturator sign
Low-grade fever, tachycardia
Digital rectal exam if diagnosis unclear (tenderness on right)
Assess for peritonism/generalised guarding suggesting perforation', 'CBC (leukocytosis with neutrophilia)
CRP (raised, supports diagnosis)
Urinalysis (to exclude UTI/renal colic as differential)
Ultrasound abdomen (first-line imaging, especially in women/children)
CT abdomen if diagnosis remains uncertain (higher sensitivity in adults)
Urine pregnancy test in women of reproductive age', NULL, 'Keep nil by mouth, IV fluids, analgesia (don''t withhold analgesia for fear of masking signs — outdated concept)
IV antibiotics (broad-spectrum covering gram-negative and anaerobic organisms) pre-operatively
Urgent surgical referral for appendicectomy (laparoscopic preferred)
If perforated/abscess — may need drainage ± interval appendicectomy
Post-op: continue antibiotics if perforated, early mobilisation, wound care

PRESCRIPTION:
IV Ceftriaxone + Metronidazole (pre-op empirical) Rocephin/Cefaxone + Flagyl Ceftriaxone 1–2g IV OD + Metronidazole 500mg IV TDS. Given pre-operatively as prophylaxis/treatment
IV Paracetamol / Diclofenac for analgesia Perfalgan/Voltral 1g IV QDS (paracetamol) / 75mg IM (diclofenac). Adequate analgesia does not obscure surgical diagnosis
Post-op oral antibiotics (if perforated) Augmentin (Co-amoxiclav) + Flagyl 625mg TDS + 400mg TDS for 5–7 days. Only continued if perforation/peritoneal contamination found at surgery', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Generalised abdominal rigidity/guarding — suspect perforation with peritonitis, surgical emergency
High fever with hypotension/tachycardia — signs of sepsis
Pregnant woman with RIF pain — urgent surgical + obstetric review
Delayed presentation (>72h) with mass — may be appendiceal abscess, needs imaging-guided management

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG141 (Suspected acute appendicitis) and WSES (World Society of Emergency Surgery) guidelines: use a validated clinical scoring system (e.g. Alvarado) combined with imaging to reduce negative appendicectomy rates; laparoscopic appendicectomy is preferred over open where expertise/resources allow; uncomplicated appendicitis may be considered for antibiotic-first management in select patients per shared decision-making, though surgery remains standard of care.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
This case needs same-day surgical referral — do not manage as pure OPD case. Educate patient on wound care, early ambulation post-op, and when to return (fever, wound discharge, worsening pain).', NULL, true, false),
('Inguinal Hernia', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Groin lump, Elective, Reducible', 'A groin lump that bulges with cough — common in labourers and the elderly.', 'Groin swelling, worse on standing/straining/coughing, reduces on lying down
Dragging discomfort or ache, especially after heavy lifting/manual labour
Ask about chronic cough, constipation, straining at urination (BPH) — precipitating factors
Duration, any episode of irreducibility or severe pain (concerning for strangulation)
Occupational history (heavy lifting) common in Pakistani labourers', 'Visible/palpable groin swelling, cough impulse present
Reducibility — can it be pushed back manually?
Differentiate inguinal vs femoral hernia (femoral below inguinal ligament, higher strangulation risk)
Check for tenderness, warmth, discolouration (signs of strangulation)
Examine both sides; assess for scrotal extension (inguino-scrotal hernia)', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis
Ultrasound groin if diagnosis uncertain (differentiate from lymphadenopathy, lipoma, hydrocele)
Routine pre-op bloods once surgery planned (CBC, renal function, clotting, blood grouping)
Assess fitness for surgery/anaesthesia (ECG, CXR if indicated by age/comorbidity)', NULL, 'Elective referral for surgical repair (mesh repair reduces recurrence)
Advise avoiding heavy lifting/straining until repaired
Treat contributing factors: chronic cough (smoking cessation), constipation (dietary fibre), BPH symptoms (urology referral)
If strangulated/obstructed — emergency surgical referral same day
Post-op: analgesia, early mobilisation, avoid heavy lifting for 4–6 weeks

PRESCRIPTION:
Simple analgesia for discomfort pre-op Panadol/Brufen (Ibuprofen) Paracetamol 1g QDS or Ibuprofen 400mg TDS PRN. For symptomatic relief while awaiting elective repair
Stool softener if constipation is a contributing strain factor Duphalac (Lactulose) 15ml OD–BD. Reduces straining and hernia symptoms
Post-op antibiotics if mesh infection risk factors Augmentin 625mg TDS for 5 days. Not routinely needed for clean elective hernia surgery', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Irreducible, tender, red/discoloured hernia with vomiting → strangulated hernia, surgical emergency
Sudden severe pain with an inability to reduce previously reducible hernia
Signs of bowel obstruction (distension, absolute constipation, vomiting)
Femoral hernia — higher strangulation risk, refer promptly even if asymptomatic

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE guidance and European Hernia Society guidelines: symptomatic inguinal hernias should be offered elective surgical repair (open Lichtenstein mesh repair or laparoscopic, based on patient/surgeon factors); watchful waiting may be reasonable for minimally symptomatic hernias in low-risk patients but most eventually need repair; strangulation/obstruction requires emergency surgery.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Avoid heavy lifting and straining, treat chronic cough/constipation, seek immediate care if the lump becomes painful, red, or irreducible — this is an emergency.', NULL, true, false),
('Haemorrhoids (Piles)', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Rectal bleed, Anal, Common', 'Bright red rectal bleeding on wiping — very common but always confirm it isn''t something worse.', 'Painless bright red bleeding on defecation, often coating stool or on tissue paper
Prolapsing lump per rectum, may reduce spontaneously or need manual reduction
Itching, mucus discharge, discomfort
Chronic constipation/straining, prolonged toilet sitting, low-fibre diet
Pregnancy (raised intra-abdominal pressure) as a common precipitant', 'Perianal inspection: external haemorrhoids visible, prolapse on straining
Digital rectal examination — internal haemorrhoids not usually palpable unless thrombosed
Proctoscopy for direct visualisation and grading (I–IV)
Assess for signs of anaemia if bleeding significant/chronic
Exclude fissure, fistula, or perianal abscess as alternate cause of symptoms', 'Proctoscopy/anoscopy for grading
CBC if chronic bleeding suspected (anaemia)
Colonoscopy if age >45–50 or alarm features present, to exclude malignancy
Routine bloods if surgery being planned', NULL, 'High-fibre diet, adequate fluid intake, avoid straining/prolonged toilet sitting
Topical haemorrhoidal creams/suppositories for symptomatic relief
Grade I–II: consider rubber band ligation in clinic if available
Grade III–IV or failed conservative treatment: refer for surgical haemorrhoidectomy
Sitz baths for comfort, especially post-procedure

PRESCRIPTION:
Topical haemorrhoid cream (steroid + local anaesthetic) Xyloproct/Anovate/Proctosedyl Apply BD–TDS for 7–10 days. Short courses only if steroid-containing to avoid skin atrophy
Lactulose (stool softener) Duphalac 15 ml OD–BD. Reduces straining
Oral flavonoid/venotonic (if available) Daflon 500 mg BD for acute episode, then OD. Reduces bleeding/inflammation in acute flares
Simple analgesia Panadol/Brufen PRN. Avoid opioids (worsen constipation)', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Change in bowel habit, weight loss, tenesmus, or dark/altered blood → colorectal cancer must be excluded, refer for colonoscopy especially if age >45–50
Severe pain with a hard, tender, bluish perianal lump → thrombosed external haemorrhoid, needs urgent assessment
Significant/persistent bleeding causing anaemia

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per ASCRS (American Society of Colon and Rectal Surgeons) and NICE CKS guidance: grade I–II haemorrhoids managed conservatively (fibre, fluids, topical agents) ± office procedures (rubber band ligation); grade III–IV or failed conservative management considered for surgical haemorrhoidectomy or stapled haemorrhoidopexy; always rule out other causes of rectal bleeding before attributing symptoms to haemorrhoids, especially over age 45.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Increase dietary fibre and water intake, avoid straining and prolonged sitting on the toilet, warm sitz baths after bowel movements, seek review if bleeding persists, changes character, or is associated with weight loss.', NULL, true, false),
('Anal Fissure', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Anal pain, Constipation, Bleeding', 'Excruciating pain with defecation — patients fear going to the toilet.', 'Severe sharp pain during and after defecation (''like passing glass''), lasting minutes to hours
Bright red bleeding, usually small amount on the stool surface/tissue
History of hard stools/constipation preceding onset
Fear of defecation leading to stool withholding, worsening constipation cycle
Chronicity — recurrent episodes suggest chronic fissure', 'Gentle perianal inspection (often diagnostic without DRE, which may not be tolerated acutely)
Posterior midline tear most common; anterior or multiple/lateral fissures atypical — consider Crohn''s/TB/HIV
Sentinel skin tag or hypertrophied papilla in chronic fissure
Increased anal sphincter tone often palpable/visible
Avoid forceful DRE in acute painful fissure', 'Usually clinical diagnosis, no imaging needed initially
Consider colonoscopy/further workup if atypical location, recurrent, or systemic symptoms present
CBC if chronic bleeding', NULL, 'High-fibre diet and adequate hydration to soften stool
Topical GTN 0.4% or Diltiazem 2% ointment applied to anal margin BD for 6–8 weeks
Warm sitz baths after bowel movements for symptomatic relief
Adequate analgesia to break the pain-constipation-tear cycle
If chronic/refractory after 6–8 weeks: refer for botulinum toxin injection or lateral sphincterotomy

PRESCRIPTION:
GTN ointment 0.4% (topical) Rectogesic Pea-sized amount to anal margin BD–TDS for 6–8 weeks. Warn about headache as common side effect
Diltiazem 2% ointment (alternative to GTN) Compounded/available via pharmacy Apply BD for 6–8 weeks. Fewer headaches than GTN, good alternative
Lactulose / Isabgol (Psyllium husk) Duphalac/Ispaghol husk 15ml OD–BD (lactulose) or 1 tbsp in water OD (isabgol). Softens stool, prevents recurrence
Simple analgesia Panadol/Brufen PRN. Avoid opioids (constipating)', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Fissures off the midline (lateral) or multiple fissures → consider Crohn''s disease, TB, or immunosuppression, investigate further
Non-healing fissure despite adequate treatment → consider biopsy to exclude malignancy
Associated systemic symptoms (weight loss, diarrhoea) suggesting IBD

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS and ASCRS guidelines: first-line management is conservative — stool softeners, fibre, sitz baths, and topical GTN (glyceryl trinitrate) or calcium channel blocker (diltiazem) ointment to relax the internal sphincter and promote healing; botulinum toxin injection or lateral internal sphincterotomy reserved for chronic fissures failing medical therapy.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
High-fibre diet, plenty of water, warm sitz baths after each bowel motion, continue ointment for the full 6–8 weeks even after pain settles, review if not improving to consider procedural options.', NULL, true, false),
('Diabetic Foot Ulcer', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Ulcer, Diabetes, Limb-threatening', 'A limb-threatening complication of poorly controlled diabetes — check every diabetic''s feet, every visit.', 'Painless or minimally painful wound (due to neuropathy) noticed by patient or family
Duration of diabetes, glycaemic control history, prior ulcers/amputations
Reduced sensation, tingling, numbness in feet (''stocking'' pattern)
Claudication or rest pain (suggests coexisting peripheral arterial disease)
Trauma history, footwear habits, barefoot walking', 'Inspect for ulcer site, size, depth, base (granulating vs sloughy vs necrotic), surrounding cellulitis
Assess sensation with 10g monofilament and vibration sense
Palpate peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) — absent suggests ischaemia
Look for deformity (Charcot foot), callus, nail changes
Probe-to-bone test if ulcer is deep (suggests osteomyelitis)', 'Random/fasting glucose and HbA1c
Wound swab/culture if infected
X-ray foot to assess for osteomyelitis or Charcot changes
Ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) or Doppler if pulses reduced/absent
CBC, CRP/ESR if systemic infection suspected', NULL, 'Urgent referral to diabetic foot/vascular surgery clinic for any active ulcer
Debridement of non-viable tissue, appropriate wound dressing, offloading (special footwear/total contact cast)
Optimise glycaemic control (may need insulin even in previously oral-agent-controlled patients during active infection)
Broad-spectrum antibiotics guided by culture/severity; escalate for suspected osteomyelitis
Vascular assessment/revascularisation if ischaemic component present
Patient education: daily foot inspection, well-fitted footwear, never walk barefoot

PRESCRIPTION:
Augmentin (Co-amoxiclav) — mild/moderate infection Augmentin/Avlox/Clavamox 625 mg TDS for 7–14 days (oral) or 1.2g IV TDS if inpatient. Cover skin/soft tissue and some gram-negative organisms
Clindamycin (if penicillin allergic) Dalacin C 300–450 mg QDS. Alternative in penicillin allergy
Insulin (short-term, for glycaemic optimisation during active infection) Humulin R/Novorapid Sliding scale/basal-bolus per glucose monitoring. Often needed temporarily even if usually on oral agents
Appropriate wound dressing (e.g. hydrocolloid/foam) As per wound care supply Change per wound care protocol. Selected based on exudate level, not one-size-fits-all', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Spreading cellulitis, crepitus, foul odour, systemic sepsis signs → limb-threatening infection, urgent surgical/vascular referral, IV antibiotics
Absent pulses with rest pain/gangrene → critical limb ischaemia, urgent vascular referral
Positive probe-to-bone test → suspect osteomyelitis, needs imaging and prolonged antibiotics
Rapidly progressive Charcot foot changes

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG19 (Diabetic foot problems) and IWGDF (International Working Group on the Diabetic Foot) guidelines: all diabetics should have annual foot risk stratification; any active ulcer needs referral to a multidisciplinary diabetic foot team within 24 hours (if infected/limb-threatening) or 1 week (if not); offload the wound (pressure relief), debride non-viable tissue, treat infection based on culture, and address vascular supply — treating glucose control alone is not sufficient.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Daily foot self-inspection (use a mirror if needed), never walk barefoot, well-fitted diabetic footwear, urgent review for any new redness/discharge/odour, strict glycaemic control, do not delay — early referral saves limbs.', NULL, true, false),
('Renal Colic (Urolithiasis)', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Loin pain, Stones, Acute', 'Sudden severe loin-to-groin pain — patients can''t sit still, unlike peritonitis.', 'Sudden severe colicky loin pain radiating to groin/testis/labia, patient writhing/restless (unlike peritonitis where they lie still)
Nausea, vomiting, haematuria (visible or microscopic)
Fever/rigors — suggests infected obstructed stone (urgent)
Previous stone episodes, family history, dietary factors (low fluid intake, high in Pakistan''s hot climate, high oxalate/purine diet)
Urinary frequency/urgency if stone near vesicoureteric junction', 'Patient restless, unable to find comfortable position
Renal angle tenderness
Check for fever, tachycardia, hypotension (sepsis signs)
Abdominal exam to exclude other causes of acute abdomen
Urinalysis at bedside for blood/nitrites/leucocytes', 'Urinalysis (haematuria common, check for infection)
CBC, renal function (urea/creatinine), electrolytes
Non-contrast CT KUB (gold standard for stone detection) or renal ultrasound if CT unavailable/contraindicated (pregnancy)
Urine culture if infection suspected', NULL, 'First-line analgesia: NSAIDs (IM/PR/IV diclofenac) unless contraindicated; add opioids if needed
Antiemetics for nausea/vomiting
Encourage oral fluids once vomiting settles; IV fluids if dehydrated
Medical expulsive therapy (tamsulosin) for stones ≤10mm to aid passage
Urgent urology referral if infected obstructed system, uncontrolled pain, AKI, or solitary/transplant kidney
Strain urine for stone retrieval and analysis if possible

PRESCRIPTION:
Diclofenac Voltral/Dicloran 75 mg IM stat, then 50 mg BD–TDS oral. First-line for renal colic per NICE; caution in renal impairment
Tamsulosin (medical expulsive therapy) Flomax/Contiflo 0.4 mg OD for up to 4 weeks. For stones ≤10mm to facilitate passage
Antiemetic Maxolon (Metoclopramide)/Gravinate 10 mg IV/IM TDS PRN. For associated nausea/vomiting
IV Ceftriaxone (if infected obstruction — urgent hospital care) Rocephin 1–2g IV OD. Only alongside urgent decompression, inpatient setting', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Fever/rigors with obstructing stone → infected obstructed system, urological emergency requiring urgent decompression (stent/nephrostomy)
Solitary kidney or bilateral obstruction with reduced urine output → urgent referral
Uncontrolled pain despite analgesia, or signs of sepsis/AKI

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG118 (Renal and ureteric stones) and EAU (European Association of Urology) guidelines: NSAIDs are first-line analgesia for renal colic (superior to opioids for pain and vomiting); offer non-contrast CT KUB within 24 hours to confirm diagnosis; stones <5mm often pass spontaneously with medical expulsive therapy (alpha-blockers); larger stones or those causing infection/obstruction need urological intervention (ureteroscopy, ESWL, or PCNL depending on size/location).

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Increase fluid intake (2–3L/day) especially in Pakistan''s hot climate, strain urine to catch passed stone for analysis, dietary modification based on stone type once analysed, return immediately if fever or inability to pass urine develops.', NULL, true, false),
('Breast Lump (Fibroadenoma / Ca Screening)', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Lump, Women''s health, Triple assessment', 'Any breast lump needs a systematic ''triple assessment'' — never dismiss it as ''just a cyst'' without workup.', 'Duration, change in size, relation to menstrual cycle (fibroadenomas often stable, cysts may fluctuate)
Nipple discharge (character, unilateral/bilateral, blood-stained), skin changes, nipple retraction
Family history of breast/ovarian cancer, previous breast disease
Age (fibroadenoma typically <30 years; malignancy risk rises with age)
Breastfeeding status if relevant (mastitis/abscess differential)', 'Inspect both breasts for asymmetry, skin dimpling (peau d''orange), nipple retraction
Palpate systematically for lump characteristics: size, mobility, consistency, tenderness
Examine axillary and supraclavicular lymph nodes
Check for nipple discharge on gentle expression
Examine the contralateral breast for comparison', 'Triple assessment: clinical exam + imaging (ultrasound <35 years, mammography ± ultrasound if ≥35–40 years) + core needle biopsy/FNAC if indicated
Ultrasound-guided core biopsy for any suspicious solid lesion
Routine bloods only if surgery planned', NULL, 'Any discrete new lump → refer for triple assessment (do not just reassure clinically)
If confirmed simple fibroadenoma <3cm on triple assessment in a young patient — can be managed conservatively with review, or excised if patient preference/growing
Cysts — aspirate if symptomatic; refer if blood-stained aspirate or residual mass
Confirmed malignancy — refer to breast oncology multidisciplinary team for staging and treatment planning
Provide psychological support and clear communication throughout the pathway, given cultural anxiety around breast lumps in Pakistan

PRESCRIPTION:
Simple analgesia if tender Panadol/Brufen PRN. Symptomatic only while awaiting assessment
If mastitis/abscess suspected (lactational) Augmentin (Co-amoxiclav) 625 mg TDS for 5–7 days. Continue breastfeeding/expressing; drain if abscess confirmed on ultrasound', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Hard, irregular, fixed lump, skin dimpling, nipple retraction, bloody nipple discharge, axillary lymphadenopathy → urgent 2-week-wait breast cancer referral
New lump in a woman >40, especially with family history
Peau d''orange or inflammatory changes (may mimic mastitis — inflammatory breast cancer must be excluded if not resolving with antibiotics)

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE NG12 (Suspected cancer referral) guidance: refer on a suspected-cancer pathway (urgent, seen within 2 weeks) for an unexplained breast lump in anyone ≥30, or unilateral nipple changes/skin changes in those ≥50; use the ''triple assessment'' approach — clinical, radiological, pathological — before reassuring a patient that a lump is benign; a normal exam alone is not sufficient to exclude cancer.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Emphasise that most breast lumps are benign but every new lump needs proper triple assessment before reassurance, not reassurance without workup. Encourage breast awareness and timely reporting of any changes; address anxiety/stigma sensitively.', NULL, true, false),
('Cellulitis', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Skin infection, Red leg, Common', 'A hot, red, swollen leg — common, but the line between ''oral antibiotics at home'' and ''admit for IV'' matters.', 'Onset, duration, and speed of spread of redness/swelling
Pain, warmth, firmness of the affected area
Systemic symptoms: fever, chills, malaise, confusion (especially in elderly)
Portal of entry: skin breach, ulcer, fungal infection (tinea pedis), eczema, insect bite, IV cannula site
Comorbidities: diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, immunosuppression, lymphoedema, chronic venous insufficiency', 'Mark the margin of redness with a pen to track spread over time
Assess for systemic signs: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, confusion
Check distal pulses and sensation (rule out compartment syndrome or arterial compromise)
Look for the portal of entry (interdigital fungal infection, ulcer, trauma)
Differentiate from other diagnoses: abscess (fluctuant), DVT (no skin break, calf tenderness), necrotising fasciitis (severe pain out of proportion, rapid spread, systemic toxicity)', 'Usually a clinical diagnosis — no investigations needed for uncomplicated cellulitis
CBC, CRP, blood cultures if systemically unwell/febrile
Wound swab if open wound/ulcer with purulent discharge
Imaging (X-ray/MRI) only if deep infection, osteomyelitis, or necrotising fasciitis suspected', NULL, 'Mild/moderate, no systemic signs: oral antibiotics, mark the erythema margin, review in 48 hours
Severe, systemic signs, or high-risk (diabetic, immunocompromised): admit for IV antibiotics
Treat the portal of entry (tinea pedis, ulcer, eczema) to prevent recurrence
Elevate the affected limb to reduce swelling
Analgesia and hydration
Safety-net advice: return immediately if redness spreads rapidly, severe pain develops, or systemic symptoms worsen

PRESCRIPTION:
Flucloxacillin (first-line oral) Floxaclox/Cloxin 500mg QDS for 5-7 days. First-line per NICE/IDSA; covers Strep and Staph aureus; take on empty stomach
Co-amoxiclav (alternative if penicillin-allergic but not anaphylactic, or broader cover needed) Augmentin 625mg TDS for 5-7 days. Broader spectrum, covers anaerobes too
Clindamycin (penicillin-allergic) Dalacin-C 300-450mg QDS for 5-7 days. Good tissue penetration, covers Strep and Staph; alternative for penicillin-allergic patients', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Rapidly spreading erythema with severe pain out of proportion to visible signs, systemic toxicity, skin necrosis/bruising, crepitus → suspect necrotising fasciitis, SURGICAL EMERGENCY
Sepsis signs (hypotension, tachycardia, confusion, high lactate) → admit, IV antibiotics, resuscitation
Severe cellulitis in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient → admit for IV antibiotics
Facial cellulitis (periorbital/orbital) → admit, risk of intracranial spread
Non-response to oral antibiotics after 48-72 hours → reassess, consider IV or resistant organism

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CKS (Cellulitis — acute) and IDSA skin infection guidelines: use the Eron classification to assess severity and site of care; most uncomplicated cellulitis (no systemic signs, no comorbidity) can be managed with oral antibiotics in the community; oral flucloxacillin is first-line (covers Streptococcus and Staphylococcus aureus); IV antibiotics reserved for severe/systemic illness, failure of oral therapy, or high-risk patients; mark the erythema margin to monitor response.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Mark the red edge with a pen to track if it''s spreading or shrinking, elevate the limb, complete the full antibiotic course, treat any athlete''s foot or skin break to prevent recurrence, return immediately if redness spreads rapidly or you feel more unwell.', NULL, true, false),
('Varicose Veins', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Veins, Leg swelling, Common', 'Twisted, bulging veins — usually a quality-of-life issue, but the real question is whether there''s something more concerning underneath.', 'Visible twisted, dilated veins, usually in lower legs
Symptoms: aching, heaviness, itching, swelling (worse end of day, relieved by elevation/rest)
Skin changes: eczema, lipodermatosclerosis, hyperpigmentation, venous ulceration
Bleeding from varices, superficial thrombophlebitis (tender hard lump along vein)
Risk factors: prolonged standing, pregnancy, obesity, family history, age', 'Inspect for visible varicosities, distribution (long/short saphenous system)
Skin changes: haemosiderin staining, lipodermatosclerosis, eczema, ulceration (gaiter area)
Palpate for tenderness, induration (superficial thrombophlebitis)
Tap test (cough impulse) to assess saphenofemoral junction incompetence
Check arterial pulses (coexisting arterial disease affects management)', 'Clinical diagnosis often sufficient for uncomplicated varicose veins
Venous duplex ultrasound (Duplex) — the key investigation to map venous anatomy, reflux, and deep vein patency; guides management
ABPI (ankle-brachial pressure index) if arterial disease suspected or before compression therapy', NULL, 'Conservative: lifestyle advice (weight loss, exercise, leg elevation, avoid prolonged standing)
Compression stockings (Class I or II) for symptom relief if not suitable for intervention
Interventional treatment (NICE-recommended if symptomatic with reflux on duplex): endothermal ablation (radiofrequency or laser) first-line, sclerotherapy (foam or liquid) second-line, open surgery (high tie + stripping) if ablation unsuitable
Treat skin changes/ulceration aggressively — refer to leg ulcer/dressings clinic
Follow-up post-treatment to assess for recurrence

PRESCRIPTION:
Compression stockings (Class II) Jobst/Sigvaris/Medi Put on before getting out of bed, worn during day. Symptomatic relief, not curative; ensure ABPI checked before prescribing if any arterial disease suspicion
Endothermal ablation (specialist procedure) Radiofrequency (VNUS Closure)/Laser (EVLA) Day-case, local anaesthetic. NICE first-line interventional treatment; faster recovery than open surgery
Foam sclerotherapy (specialist procedure) Sodium tetradecyl sulphate (Fibrovein) Ultrasound-guided injection. Second-line per NICE; suitable for smaller veins or recurrent varicosities
Simple analgesia Panadol/Brufen PRN. For symptomatic aching/discomfort', 'RED FLAGS / URGENT REFERRAL:
Ulceration with surrounding cellulitis or rapid enlargement → urgent vascular referral
Bleeding varix (can be profuse) → direct pressure, elevation, urgent review
Superficial thrombophlebitis near saphenofemoral junction → risk of DVT/extension, needs imaging and anticoagulation consideration
Suspected DVT (calf swelling/tenderness, unilateral) → exclude first, varicosities alone don''t cause significant swelling without complication

GUIDELINE SUMMARY:
Per NICE CG168 (Varicose veins in the legs): interventional treatment (endothermal ablation, sclerotherapy, or surgery) recommended for symptomatic varicose veins with confirmed saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal reflux on duplex; conservative management (compression stockings) only if interventional treatment unsuitable or declined; do NOT offer compression hosiery as a ''treatment'' to prevent recurrence if no symptoms present; treat underlying superficial reflux, not just visible veins.

PATIENT ADVICE & FOLLOW-UP:
Stay active, elevate legs when resting, avoid prolonged standing, wear compression stockings if prescribed, seek urgent review if a vein bleeds, becomes suddenly painful/hard, or a leg ulcer develops.', NULL, true, false),
('Thyroid Nodule', 'Surgery', NULL, NULL, 'Neck lump, Thyroid, Cancer screening', 'A lump in the neck found incidentally or on self-examination — most are benign, but the workup is designed to find the few that aren''t.', 'Neck lump noticed by patient or found incidentally on imaging
Rate of growth, pain, pressure symptoms (dysphagia, dyspnoea, voice change/hoarseness — concerning for malignancy)
History of neck radiation, family history of thyroid cancer (MEN2, familial medullary thyroid cancer)
Age and sex (higher malignancy risk in men, very young, and elderly), history of childhood irradiation
Symptoms of hyperthyroidism (palpitations, weight loss, heat intolerance) or hypothyroidism', 'Palpate the nodule: size, consistency (hard/irregular/fixed suggests malignancy), mobility with swallowing
Assess for cervical lymphadenopathy (concerning for metastatic spread)
Examine for signs of hyperthyroidism (tremor, tachycardia, eye signs) or hypothyroidism
Assess voice/hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement)', 'Thyroid function tests (TSH first-line, then free T4/T3 if abnormal)
Thyroid ultrasound — characterises nodule (solid vs cystic, features suggesting malignancy: microcalcification, irregular margins, taller-than-wide, hypoechogenicity)
Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) — for nodules meeting biopsy criteria (based on size and ultrasound features); U classification (U1-U5) guides management
Calcitonin if medullary thyroid cancer suspected (family history, MEN2)', NULL, 'Reassure — most thyroid nodules are benign
If thyroid function abnormal — treat the underlying thyroid dysfunction
If nodule meets biopsy criteria — FNAC for cytology
If cytology malignant/indeterminate — refer to thyroid MDT for surgery (hemithyroidectomy or total thyroidectomy depending on findings)
If benign and asymptomatic — serial ultrasound surveillance, no intervention needed
If symptomatic benign nodule (pressure symptoms) — consider surgical excision

PRESCRIPTION:
Levothyroxine (if hypothyroid) Thyroxine/Eltroxin 25-100mcg OD, titrate to TSH. Treat underlying hypothyroidism if present
Carbimazole (if hyperthyroid) Neo-Mercazole 5-20mg OD-BD, titrate to thyroid function. Treat underlying hyperthyroidism if present
